  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and cool conditions continue through \ntonight, then temperatures gradually warm to seasonable values \nby Wednesday.\n\nThe primary focus for the rest of the week will continue to be \nthe high pressure system that will slowly expand into the \nregion. Expect overnight lows to be a tad warmer with temps. in \nthe mid to upper 30s across the inland and low to mid 40s along \nthe coastal corridor. We've nudged the overnight temperatures \ndown 1-2 degrees based off recent forecast trends. Breezy \nnortheasterly winds will continue through the evening with \nspeeds 10 to 15 mph with gusts up to 20 mph (highest along the \ncoastline). Temperatures will finally warm back up to seasonable\nvalues on Wednesday with afternoon highs ranging in the low 70s\nacross the region. We could see periods of showers develop \nwithin a nearby coastal trough, it's possible these showers \ncould shift onshore at times (esp. across the the southeast \nGeorgia coastline). The best chance for precipitation will be \nmid to late week timeframe. Regardless, no significant rainfall \namounts are expected through Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An area of low pressure could develop off the \nSoutheast coast and bring much needed widespread rainfall to \nsoutheast Georgia and southeast South Carolina late this week \nand into this weekend. \n\nThere is considerable forecast uncertainty heading into late \nthis week and into this weekend. Global forecast models are \ntrending towards a surface low pressure system developing near \nthe Atlantic coast of Florida and tracking closer to the \nSoutheast coast. While the timing, proximity to the coast, and \npotential inland extent of any rainfall are details that will \nneed to be ironed out at a later forecast update, there is \nincreasing potential for a widespread rainfall event. Rainfall \nis much needed across southeastern SC and southeastern GA, with \nall three of our local climate sites (KCHS, Downtown Charleston,\nand KSAV) on track for the 2nd driest years on record.",
